### What is the JDK Flow API?

The `Flow` class is a container for four nested interfaces added in Java 9. They are the JDK's standard adoption of the Reactive Streams specification, which defines a protocol for asynchronous stream processing with non-blocking backpressure.

### What is a Publisher?

A `Flow.Publisher<T>` is a source of items with a single method `subscribe(Subscriber<? super T> s)`. When you subscribe, the publisher hands back a `Subscription` by calling the subscriber's `onSubscribe` method.

```java
package com.bank.reactive;

import java.util.concurrent.Flow;
import java.util.concurrent.SubmissionPublisher;

public class TransactionFeedDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        SubmissionPublisher<String> publisher = new SubmissionPublisher<>();
        publisher.subscribe(new AuditSubscriber());

        publisher.submit("TXN-001: Debit Rs.50000 from SAV2001");
        publisher.submit("TXN-002: Credit Rs.50000 to CUR3001");
        publisher.close();

        Thread.sleep(1000); // let async processing complete
    }
}
```

### What is a Subscriber?

A `Flow.Subscriber<T>` has four callbacks. `onSubscribe` receives the subscription and sets initial demand. `onNext` delivers one item. `onError` is called once if the stream fails. `onComplete` is called once when the stream ends normally.

```java
package com.bank.reactive;

import java.util.concurrent.Flow;

public class AuditSubscriber implements Flow.Subscriber<String> {

    private Flow.Subscription subscription;

    @Override
    public void onSubscribe(Flow.Subscription subscription) {
        this.subscription = subscription;
        subscription.request(1);  // start with demand for 1 item
    }

    @Override
    public void onNext(String item) {
        System.out.println("Auditing: " + item);
        subscription.request(1);  // request next item after processing
    }

    @Override
    public void onError(Throwable throwable) {
        System.err.println("Stream error: " + throwable.getMessage());
    }

    @Override
    public void onComplete() {
        System.out.println("Audit stream completed.");
    }
}
```

```text
Auditing: TXN-001: Debit Rs.50000 from SAV2001
Auditing: TXN-002: Credit Rs.50000 to CUR3001
Audit stream completed.
```

### What is a Subscription?

A `Flow.Subscription` is the private channel between one publisher and one subscriber. `request(long n)` tells the publisher the subscriber is ready for `n` more items. `cancel()` tells the publisher to stop sending.

### What is a Processor?

A `Flow.Processor<T,R>` is both a subscriber and a publisher. It consumes items of type T and republishes transformed items of type R. Processors let you build pipelines like: raw feed, then fee enrichment, then fraud scoring.

```java
package com.bank.reactive;

import java.util.concurrent.Flow;
import java.util.concurrent.SubmissionPublisher;

public class FeeEnrichmentProcessor
        extends SubmissionPublisher<String>
        implements Flow.Processor<String, String> {

    private Flow.Subscription subscription;

    @Override
    public void onSubscribe(Flow.Subscription subscription) {
        this.subscription = subscription;
        subscription.request(1);
    }

    @Override
    public void onNext(String item) {
        String enriched = item + " [Fee: Rs.10]";
        submit(enriched);           // publish downstream
        subscription.request(1);    // request next from upstream
    }

    @Override
    public void onError(Throwable throwable) {
        closeExceptionally(throwable);
    }

    @Override
    public void onComplete() {
        close();
    }
}
```

### How does demand-based backpressure work?

The model is a hybrid of pull and push. The subscriber pulls by calling `request(n)` to state demand. Once demand exists, the publisher pushes items via `onNext` without the subscriber asking individually. The publisher must never send more `onNext` calls than the total requested.

### What is SubmissionPublisher?

The JDK ships `SubmissionPublisher<T>`, a ready-made `Publisher` backed by a bounded buffer and a `ForkJoinPool`. You rarely write a publisher from scratch. You use `SubmissionPublisher` and call its `submit(item)` method, and it handles the `request(n)` accounting and buffering.

### How is Flow API different from a plain Observer or Stream?

A plain Observer has no backpressure. A Java Stream is pull-based and synchronous. The Flow API adds demand-based backpressure so a fast producer never overwhelms a slow consumer.

| Feature | Observer | Java Stream | Flow API |
|---------|----------|------------|----------|
| Backpressure | No | No | Yes, via request(n) |
| Async | Depends | No | Yes |
| Operators | No | map, filter | None (use Reactor/RxJava) |

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How does SubmissionPublisher handle slow subscribers?
- What is the difference between hot and cold publishers?
- How does the Flow API relate to Reactor's Flux and Mono?
